[7.830481, "o", "1:1 Overview\r\n2:**********\r\n3:\r\n4:GNU ed is a line-oriented text editor. It is used to create, display, modify\r\n5:and otherwise manipulate text files, both interactively and via shell\r\n6:scripts. A restricted version of ed, red, can only edit files in the current\r\n7:directory and cannot execute shell commands. Ed is the 'standard' text\r\n8:editor in the sense that it is the original editor for Unix, and thus widely\r\n9:available. For most purposes, however, it is superseded by full-screen\r\n10:editors such as GNU Emacs or GNU Moe.\r\n11:\r\n12:   GNU ed is based on the editor algorithm described in Brian W. Kernighan\r\n13:and P. J. Plauger's book \"Software Tools in Pascal\", Addison-Wesley, 1981.\r\n14:\r\n15:   If invoked with a FILE argument, then a copy of FILE is read into the\r\n16:editor's buffer. Changes are made to this copy and not directly to FILE\r\n17:itself. Upon quitting 'ed', any changes not explicitly saved with a 'w'\r\n18:command are lost. In interactive mode, a non-existing FILE is reported but\r\n19:does not a"]
[7.830526, "o", "lter the exit status.\r\n20:\r\n21:   Editing is done in two distinct modes: \"command\" and \"input\". When first\r\n22:invoked, 'ed' is in command mode. In this mode commands are read from the\r\n23:standard input and executed to manipulate the contents of the editor\r\n24:buffer. A typical command might look like:\r\n25:\r\n26:     ,s/OLD/NEW/g\r\n27:\r\n28:   which replaces all occurences of the string OLD with NEW.\r\n29:\r\n30:   When an input command, such as 'a' (append), 'i' (insert) or 'c'\r\n31:(change), is given, 'ed' enters input mode. This is the primary means of\r\n32:adding text to a file. In this mode, no commands are available; instead,\r\n33:the standard input is written directly to the editor buffer. A \"line\"\r\n34:consists of the text up to and including a <newline> character. Input mode\r\n35:is terminated by entering a single period ('.') on a line.\r\n36:\r\n37:   All 'ed' commands operate on whole lines or ranges of lines; e.g., the\r\n38:'d' command deletes lines; the 'm' command moves lines, and so on. It is\r\n39:possible to"]
[7.830543, "o", " modify only a portion of a line by means of replacement, as in\r\n40:the example above. However even here, the 's' command is applied to whole\r\n41:lines at a time.\r\n42:\r\n43:   In general, 'ed' commands consist of zero or more line addresses,\r\n44:followed by a single character command and possibly additional parameters;\r\n45:i.e., commands have the structure:\r\n46:\r\n47:     [ADDRESS[,ADDRESS]]COMMAND[PARAMETERS]\r\n48:\r\n49:   The ADDRESSes indicate the line or range of lines to be affected by the\r\n50:command. If fewer addresses are given than the command accepts, then\r\n51:default addresses are supplied.\r\n52:\r\n53:2 Introduction to line editing\r\n54:******************************\r\n55:\r\n56:'ed' was created, along with the Unix operating system, by Ken Thompson and\r\n57:Dennis Ritchie. It is the refinement of its more complex, programmable\r\n58:predecessor, 'QED', to which Thompson and Ritchie had already added pattern\r\n59:matching capabilities (*note Regular expressions::).\r\n60:\r\n61:   For the purposes of this tutorial, "]
[7.830554, "o", "a working knowledge of the Unix shell\r\n62:'sh' and the Unix file system is recommended, since 'ed' is designed to\r\n63:interact closely with them. (*Note GNU bash manual: (bash)Top, for details\r\n64:about bash).\r\n65:\r\n66:   The principal difference between line editors and display editors is\r\n67:that display editors provide instant feedback to user commands, whereas\r\n68:line editors require sometimes lengthy input before any effects are seen.\r\n69:The advantage of instant feedback, of course, is that if a mistake is made,\r\n70:it can be corrected immediately, before more damage is done. Editing in\r\n71:'ed' requires more strategy and forethought; but if you are up to the task,\r\n72:it can be quite efficient.\r\n73:\r\n74:   Much of the 'ed' command syntax is shared with other Unix utilities.\r\n75:\r\n76:   As with the shell, <RETURN> (the carriage-return key) enters a line of\r\n77:input. So when we speak of \"entering\" a command or some text in 'ed',\r\n78:<RETURN> is implied at the end of each line. Prior to typing <RETURN>"]
[7.831739, "o", ",\r\n79:corrections to the line may be made by typing either <BACKSPACE> to erase\r\n80:characters backwards, or <CONTROL>-u (i.e., hold the CONTROL key and type\r\n\u001b[?2004hroot@68ab857d1493:/app# "]
[14.964946, "i", "grep -nE '^[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)* |^Appendix|^Invoking|^Regular|^Addresses|^Commands' /app/docs/ed.txt\n"]
[14.965023, "o", "grep -nE '^[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)* |^Appendix|^Invoking|^Regular|^Addresses|^Commands' /app/docs/ed.txt\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r1:1 Overview\r\n53:2 Introduction to line editing\r\n249:3 Invoking ed\r\n331:4 Line addressing\r\n433:5 Regular expressions\r\n436:Regular expressions are patterns used in selecting text. For example, the\r\n583:6 Commands\r\n867:7 Substitute command\r\n936:8 Limitations\r\n955:9 Diagnostics\r\n"]
